As the person who originally wrote the code that does this on NAO (an
adaptation of ttyrec), I'd suggest that you take a look at what it
actually does before spouting off in newsgroups.

We save hundreds of games per day, all of which are raw tty grabs as
sent to the original player. Playback relies on the tty of another
viewer to interpret those codes in the same way as they were
interpreted by the original term. The playback engine on NAO itself
(only usable to view games in progress) does the same thing, with some
minor modification to begin playback at tty screen clear boundaries so
the whole game doesn't have to be replayed to start viewing someone's
game.

Since the original version, we've also added code to strip the IBM or
DEC graphic symbols that nethack optionally uses, although this is only
necessary if your term doesn't match the term of the person playing the
game. This is the only time the stream itself is manipulated, and it
isn't the term codes that are changed, it is the high ASCII codes used
in some term programs. In any case, this isn't necessary at all if your
term is capable of displaying the same symbols as the original player
has selected, or if the original player is not using advanced graphic
symbols in their game (as the Almighty meant nethack to be played).
This code isn't used on any of the ttyplay programs that most people
view the games with.

It has worked pretty well for the couple hundred thousand games
recorded so far, which you're more than welcome to see at
http://alt.org/nethack/

